The Debian package uses CMake's agent installation target and debhelper's
CMake build system. The agent's bootstrap configuration uses persistent Cozo
stores, so the package build requires an explicitly provisioned Cozo C API.
This keeps the external dependency visible and avoids producing a package that
installs successfully but cannot use its default configuration.

Build the source package with:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Pass the Cozo paths through the build environment, for example:

    COZO_INCLUDE_DIR=/path/to/cozo/cozo-lib-c \
    COZO_LIBRARY=/path/to/libcozo_c.so \
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

The backend is selected explicitly with `LLAMA_AGENT_BACKEND`. Supported
values are `cpu`, `cuda`, `vulkan`, and `auto`. Release builds use an explicit
backend so that the package never silently changes GPU support because of the
builder's installed toolchains:

    LLAMA_AGENT_BACKEND=vulkan \
    LLAMA_AGENT_NATIVE=off \
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

For a local development build, `auto` enables CUDA and/or Vulkan when their
development toolchains are available. CPU, CUDA, and Vulkan release packages
use mutually exclusive Debian package names: `llama-agent-cpu`,
`llama-agent-cuda`, and `llama-agent-vulkan`. Each provides the virtual
capability `llama-agent-backend` and conflicts with the other backend packages.
The development package keeps the unqualified `llama-agent` name as the CPU
default.

`LLAMA_AGENT_NATIVE` defaults to `off` so binaries from a Debian archive or
PPA remain portable. A downstream maintainer building for a controlled host
may set it to `on`.

The CUDA release package declares a dependency on `cuda-cudart-13-3`. It also
requires a compatible NVIDIA driver on the target host; the driver remains a
host-level choice because its Debian package name varies by distribution and
hardware support policy.

The resulting package installs the daemon and bootstrap helper under
/usr/sbin, client and web-adapter binaries under /usr/bin, examples under
/usr/share/doc/llama-agent/examples (including the source and Nginx example
for the Vue web client), and the systemd unit without enabling or
starting it automatically. Generate /etc/llama-agent/config.json with
llama-agent-config-bootstrap before enabling llama-agent.service.
